The widespread use of social media has drawn attention to the significant issue of user-generated online content, particularly the rapid dissemination of misinformation through private chat groups. To combat this problem, apps like WhatsApp have introduced limits on message forwarding to slow the spread of potentially harmful content. Our study aimed to understand the effectiveness of these restrictions by analyzing user behavior on WhatsApp in India, who regularly engage with suspicious or misleading content. We found that WhatsApp's restrictions have had a notable impact, reducing forwarded content by 70% and slowing its dissemination rate. However, our research also revealed two key concerns. First, the limited forwarding capabilities have led group administrators to rely on manual forwarding, allowing the spread of misinformation to continue. Second, public chains have become a new outlet for forwarding content, increasing the reach of suspicious content and allowing it to be spread more widely. Our findings suggest that while WhatsApp's restrictions have had a positive effect, they have not entirely halted the spread of misinformation. We identify several areas for further research, including the role of group administrators in disseminating content and the use of public chains in spreading misinformation. Moreover, we highlight the need for WhatsApp to develop more effective strategies to combat misinformation, such as enhancing user labeling capabilities to distinguish content as being forwarded or not. Our study provides a comprehensive understanding of the complex dynamics of online misinformation and offers practical recommendations to combat its spread. We believe our research can be applied to other apps, such as Telegram, to inform our understanding of the issue and develop effective countermeasures. Our analysis offers insights for both designers and researchers, while our recommendations can help combat misinformation effectively. We have made our dat